  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 14,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.084,
            0.371,
            0.1
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Bleaching powder is used –",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.1,
            0.723,
            0.149
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) for bleaching cotton and linen in the textile industry, for bleaching wood pulp in paper factories and for bleaching washed clothes in laundry;",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.149,
            0.652,
            0.165
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) as an oxidising agent in many chemical industries; and",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.166,
            0.519,
            0.183
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iii) to make drinking water free from germs.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.197,
            0.226,
            0.213
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Baking soda"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.212,
            0.723,
            0.279
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The baking soda is commonly used in the kitchen for making tasty crispy pakoras, etc. Sometimes it is added for faster cooking. The chemical",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " name of the compound is sodium hydrogencarbonate (NaHCO "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "). It is produced using sodium chloride as one of the raw materials.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.133,
            0.289,
            0.628,
            0.339
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_14_equation_6.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{aligned} NaCl  +  H _2 O  +  CO _2 +  NH _3 & \\rightarrow  NH _4 Cl  +  NaHCO _3 \\\\& ( Ammonium   (Sodium  \\\\&  cholricde ) \\quad  hydrogenacrobance )\\end{aligned}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.345,
            0.723,
            0.412
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Did you check the pH of sodium hydrogencarbonate in Activity 2.14? Can you correlate why it can be used to neutralise an acid? It is a mild non-corrosive basic salt. The following reaction takes place when it is heated during cooking –",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.417,
            0.481,
            0.472
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_14_equation_8.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{array}{l}2\\mathrm{NaHCO}_{3} \\xrightarrow{\\quad \\text { Heat } \\quad} \\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{CO}_{3}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+\\mathrm{CO}_{2} \\\\1(\\mathrm{Sodium} \\quad \\text { (Sodium } \\\\ \\text { hydrogencarbonate) } \\quad \\text { carbonate) }\\end{array}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.48,
            0.719,
            0.498
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Sodium hydrogencarbonate has got various uses in the household.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.1,
            0.505,
            0.302,
            0.521
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Uses of Baking soda"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.522,
            0.722,
            0.589
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) Baking powder is a mixture of baking soda (sodium hydrogen carbonate) and tartaric acid which is a weak edible acid. When baking powder is heated or mixed in water, the following reaction takes place –",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.593,
            0.629,
            0.628
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_14_equation_12.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{array}{c}\\mathrm{NaHCO_{3}+H^{+}} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CO_{2}+H_{2}O+Sodium salt of acid} \\\\ (From any acid)  \\end{array}"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.448,
            0.971,
            0.552,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.129,
            0.635,
            0.721,
            0.668
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Carbon dioxide produced during the reaction can cause bread or cake to rise making them soft and spongy.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.668,
            0.721,
            0.716
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) Sodium hydrogencarbonate is also an ingredient in antacids. Being alkaline, it neutralises excess acid in the stomach and provides relief.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.716,
            0.564,
            0.733
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iii) It is also used in soda-acid fire extinguishers.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.747,
            0.241,
            0.764
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Washing soda"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.764,
            0.724,
            0.83
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Another chemical that can be obtained from sodium chloride is",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm{Na_{2}CO_{3}.10H_{2}O}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(washing soda). You have seen above that sodium carbonate can be obtained by heating baking soda; recrystallisation of sodium carbonate gives washing soda. It is also a basic salt.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.839,
            0.453,
            0.883
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_14_equation_19.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{array}{l}\\mathrm{Na_{2}CO_{3}+10H_{2}O{\\rightarrow}Na_{2}CO_{3}.10H_{2}O} \\\\\\mathrm{(SOdim} \\\\\\mathrm{(acbounde)}\\end{array}"
        },
        {
